brief:
A tick log stores trade times in milliseconds, in order that never goes down; several trades can share a time. Analysts ask for the first and last position of a time in the log. The input comes as one string:

- the first line holds `n` and `q`, the number of ticks and of queries;
- the second line holds the `n` tick times;
- the third line holds the `q` query times.

Write `solve_tick_ranges(text)`, which returns one line per query, joined by `"\n"`. A line holds the first and last position (counting from 0), separated by a space. When the time is not in the log the line is `-1 c`, where `c` is the number of ticks before that time. With no queries return `""`. Expect extra spaces and blank lines.

Example. For `"6 2\n3 5 5 5 8 9\n5 4\n"` time 5 sits at positions 1 to 3 and 4 is absent. The answer is `"1 3\n-1 1"`.

